Article 284: Where an authority or relevant organization prescribed by laws such as the Environmental Protection Law and the Law on the Protection of Consumer Rights and Interests initiates a public interest action against conduct that pollutes the environment, infringes upon the lawful rights and interests of numerous consumers, or otherwise damages the social public interest, in accordance with Article 55 of the Civil Procedure Law, the People's Court shall accept the case if the following conditions are met:
(1) there is a definite defendant;
(2) there are specific claims;
(3) there is preliminary evidence that the social public interest has been damaged; and
(4) the case falls within the scope of civil actions accepted by the People's Court and within the jurisdiction of the accepting People's Court.

Article 295: The failure to participate in the proceedings due to reasons not attributable to oneself as provided in Article 56, paragraph 3 of the Civil Procedure Law means the circumstances where a person is not listed as a party to the effective judgment, ruling, or mediation paper and is without fault or obvious fault. Such circumstances include:
(1) not knowing of the action and therefore not participating;
(2) applying to participate but not being permitted;
(3) knowing of the action but being unable to participate due to objective reasons; and
(4) failing to participate in the proceedings due to other reasons not attributable to oneself.

Article 297: Where a third-party revocation action is initiated against any of the following circumstances, the People's Court shall not accept the case:
(1) cases handled under non-contentious procedures such as special procedures, summary procedures for urging debt repayment, public notice for exhortation procedures, and bankruptcy procedures;
(2) content involving personal status relationships in judgments, rulings, or mediation papers, such as those concerning nullity of marriage, revocation of marriage, or dissolution of marital relationship;
(3) the effective judgment or ruling on a representative action by a right holder who has not registered to participate as provided in Article 54 of the Civil Procedure Law; and
(4) the effective judgment or ruling on a public interest action by a victim of conduct damaging the social public interest as provided in Article 55 of the Civil Procedure Law.

Article 300: With respect to a third party's request to revoke or partially revoke the content of a legally effective judgment, ruling, or mediation paper, the People's Court, upon trial, shall handle the matter according to the following circumstances:
(1) if the request is established and the claim for confirmation of its civil rights is wholly or partially established, the erroneous part of the original judgment, ruling, or mediation paper shall be changed;
(2) if the request is established, but the claim for confirmation of its civil rights in whole or in part is not established, or no such claim for confirmation has been made, the erroneous part of the original judgment, ruling, or mediation paper shall be revoked;
(3) if the request is not established, the claim shall be dismissed.
If a party is dissatisfied with the judgment or ruling provided in the preceding paragraph, it may appeal.
The parts of the original judgment, ruling, or mediation paper that have not been changed or revoked shall remain valid.

Article 302: Where a third party's claims are incorporated into the retrial proceedings for trial, the matter shall be handled according to the following circumstances:
(1) if the case is tried under the first instance procedure, the People's Court shall try the third party's claims together, and the judgment rendered may be appealed;
(2) if the case is tried under the second instance procedure, the People's Court may conduct mediation; if mediation fails to reach an agreement, it shall rule to revoke the original judgment, ruling, or mediation paper and remand the case to the first instance court for retrial, and the third party shall be listed in the retrial.

Article 313: With respect to an action on enforcement objection initiated by the person applying for enforcement, the People's Court, upon trial, shall handle the matter according to the following circumstances:
(1) if the person not involved in the case does not have a civil right or interest in the subject matter of enforcement sufficient to exclude compulsory enforcement, the court shall rule to permit enforcement of that subject matter;
(2) if the person not involved in the case has a civil right or interest in the subject matter of enforcement sufficient to exclude compulsory enforcement, the court shall rule to dismiss the claim.

Article 319: Where one or some of the necessary co-litigants file an appeal, the matter shall be handled according to the following circumstances:
(1) if the appeal only concerns the allocation of rights and obligations with the opposing party and does not affect the interests of other co-litigants, the opposing party shall be the respondent, and the non-appealing party on the same side shall be listed according to its procedural status in the original trial;
(2) if the appeal only concerns the allocation of rights and obligations among the co-litigants and does not affect the interests of the opposing party, the non-appealing party on the same side shall be the respondent, and the opposing party shall be listed according to its procedural status in the original trial;
(3) if the appeal concerns the allocation of rights and obligations between the parties as well as among the co-litigants, all other parties who did not file an appeal shall be respondents.

Article 325: The following circumstances may be recognized as a serious violation of statutory procedures as provided in Article 170, paragraph 1, item 4 of the Civil Procedure Law:
(1) the composition of the trial organization is illegal;
(2) a judicial officer who should have withdrawn did not withdraw;
(3) a person without capacity for litigation did not have a statutory agent to represent him or her in the proceedings; or
(4) a party's right to debate was illegally deprived.

Article 333: The People's Court of second instance may, in accordance with Article 169 of the Civil Procedure Law, try the following appeal cases without holding a court session:
(1) appeals against rulings on non-acceptance, jurisdictional objections, and dismissal of prosecution;
(2) where the claims on appeal raised by the parties are clearly untenable;
(3) where the facts found in the original judgment or ruling are clear, but the law was incorrectly applied; or
(4) where the original judgment seriously violates statutory procedures and needs to be remanded for retrial.

Article 347 The public notice for locating the missing person shall record the following:
(1) The respondent shall report its specific address and contact information to the accepting court within the prescribed period. Otherwise, the respondent shall be declared missing or dead;
(2) Any person who knows the current status of survival of the respondent shall report what he or she knows to the accepting court within the period of the public notice.

Article 357 Where a party applies for judicial confirmation of a mediation agreement, if any of the following circumstances exists, the People's Court shall rule to refuse to accept the application:
(1) The matter is not within the scope of acceptance of the People's Court;
(2) The matter is not within the jurisdiction of the People's Court that received the application;
(3) The application seeks confirmation of the invalidity, validity, or dissolution of a status relationship such as marital relationship, parent-child relationship, or adoptive relationship;
(4) The matter involves the application of other special procedures, public notice for claims procedures, or bankruptcy procedures;
(5) The content of the mediation agreement involves the confirmation of real rights or intellectual property rights.
After accepting the application, if the People's Court discovers any of the foregoing circumstances of refusal to accept, it shall rule to dismiss the party's application.

Article 360 Upon review, if the mediation agreement falls under any of the following circumstances, the People's Court shall rule to dismiss the application:
(1) It violates mandatory provisions of law;
(2) It harms national interests, social public interests, or the lawful rights and interests of others;
(3) It violates public order and good customs;
(4) It violates the principle of voluntariness;
(5) The content is unclear;
(6) Other circumstances where judicial confirmation cannot be carried out.

Article 367 To apply for the realization of a security interest, the following materials shall be submitted:
(1) A written application. The written application shall state the basic information such as the name or title and contact information of the applicant and the respondent, the specific claim, and the facts and grounds;
(2) Materials evidencing the existence of the security interest, including the main contract, the security contract, the certificate of mortgage registration or the certificate of other rights, the certificate of rights for a pledge of rights or the certificate of registration of the pledge, etc.;
(3) Materials evidencing that the conditions for realizing the security interest have been fulfilled;
(4) A description of the current status of the security property;
(5) Other materials that the People's Court considers necessary to submit.

Article 372 After review, the People's Court shall handle the matter according to the following circumstances:
(1) If the parties have no substantive dispute on the realization of the security interest and the conditions for realizing the security interest have been fulfilled, it shall rule to permit the auction or sale of the security property;
(2) If the parties have a substantive dispute on part of the realization of the security interest, it may rule to permit the auction or sale of the security property for the undisputed part;
(3) If the parties have a substantive dispute on the realization of the security interest, it shall rule to dismiss the application and inform the applicant to file an action with the People's Court.

Article 377 A party applying for a retrial shall submit the following materials:
(1) A written application for retrial, together with copies for the number of the respondent and other parties in the original trial;
(2) If the applicant for retrial is a natural person, it shall submit its identification document; if the applicant for retrial is a legal person or other organization, it shall submit its business license, organization code certificate, and identification certificate of its legal representative or principal responsible person. If the applicant entrusts another person to act on its behalf, it shall submit the power of attorney and the identification document of the agent;
(3) The original trial judgment, ruling, or mediation statement;
(4) The main evidence reflecting the basic facts of the case and other materials.
The materials specified in items (2), (3), and (4) of the preceding paragraph may be copies that have been verified to be identical with the originals.

Article 378 The written application for retrial shall clearly state the following:
(1) The basic information of the applicant for retrial, the respondent, and other parties in the original trial;
(2) The name of the original trial People's Court, and the case number of the original adjudicative document;
(3) The specific claims for retrial;
(4) The statutory grounds for applying for retrial and the specific facts and reasons.
The written application for retrial shall specify the People's Court to which the application for retrial is made, and shall be signed, fingerprinted, or sealed by the applicant for retrial.

Article 383 Where a party applies for a retrial under any of the following circumstances, the People's Court shall not accept the application:
(1) The application is filed again after the application for retrial has been dismissed;
(2) An application is filed against a judgment or ruling rendered upon retrial;
(3) An application is filed after the People's Procuratorate has made a decision not to issue a procuratorial recommendation for retrial or a protest in response to the party's application.
For the circumstances specified in items (1) and (2) of the preceding paragraph, the People's Court shall inform the party that it may apply to the People's Procuratorate for a procuratorial recommendation for retrial or a protest, except for judgments or rulings rendered upon retrial due to a procuratorial recommendation for retrial or a protest made by the People's Procuratorate. (Interpretation reference statistics)

Article 388 Where the applicant for retrial proves that the new evidence submitted meets any of the following circumstances, the reasons for providing evidence belatedly may be deemed established:
(1) The evidence already existed before the conclusion of the original trial, but was discovered only after the conclusion of the trial due to objective reasons;
(2) The evidence was discovered before the conclusion of the original trial, but could not be obtained due to objective reasons or could not be provided within the prescribed period;
(3) The evidence was formed after the conclusion of the original trial, and no separate action can be filed based on it.
If the evidence submitted by the applicant for retrial has already been provided in the original trial, and the original trial People's Court did not organize cross-examination and did not use it as the basis for adjudication, the reasons for providing evidence belatedly shall be deemed established, except where the original trial People's Court refused to admit the evidence in accordance with Article 65 of the Civil Procedure Law.

Article 390 Where any of the following circumstances causes an erroneous result of a judgment or ruling, it shall be deemed that the original judgment or ruling has genuinely erroneous application of law as specified in Article 200, item (6) of the Civil Procedure Law:
(1) The law applied is clearly inconsistent with the nature of the case;
(2) The determination of civil liability clearly violates the agreement of the parties or the provisions of law;
(3) A law that has already ceased to have effect or has not yet come into force is applied;
(4) The provisions on the retroactive effect of law are violated;
(5) The rules on the application of law are violated;
(6) The legislative intent is clearly violated.

Article 391 Where any of the following circumstances occurs during the original trial hearing, it shall be deemed as depriving a party of the right to debate as specified in Article 200, item (9) of the Civil Procedure Law:
(1) The party is not permitted to present its arguments;
(2) The case should have been heard in court but was not heard in court;
(3) The service of the duplicate of the statement of complaint or the statement of appeal violates legal provisions, thereby preventing the party from exercising the right to debate;
(4) Other circumstances where the party's right to debate is illegally deprived.

Article 393 The legal instruments specified in Article 200, Item (12) of the Civil Procedure Law include:
(1) Judgments, rulings, and mediation statements that have become legally effective;
(2) Arbitration awards that have become legally effective;
(3) Notarized instruments of creditor's rights with compulsory execution effect.

Article 402 During the period of reviewing an application for retrial, if any of the following circumstances exists, the review shall be ruled to be terminated:
(1) The applicant for retrial dies or is dissolved, and there is no successor to rights and obligations, or the successor renounces the application for retrial;
(2) In an action for payment, the respondent who has the obligation to pay dies or is dissolved, there is no property available for execution, and there is no person who shall assume the obligation;
(3) The parties have reached a settlement agreement and it has been fully performed, except where the parties declare in the settlement agreement that they do not waive the right to apply for retrial;
(4) A third party applies for retrial in the name of a party without authorization;
(5) The original trial court or the court at the next higher level has already ruled to conduct a retrial;
(6) The circumstances specified in Article 383, Paragraph 1 of this Interpretation exist.

Article 404 When the People's Court conducts a trial in court for a retrial case, it shall proceed as follows according to the respective circumstances:
(1) If the retrial is due to a party's application, the applicant for retrial shall first state the retrial requests and grounds, followed by the respondent's defense and the opinions of other parties in the original trial;
(2) If the retrial is due to a protest, the protesting authority shall first read the protest document, then the party applying for the protest shall make a statement, followed by the respondent's defense and the opinions of other parties in the original trial;
(3) If the retrial is initiated by the People's Court ex officio and there is a petitioner, the petitioner shall first state the retrial requests and grounds, followed by the respondent's defense and the opinions of other parties in the original trial;
(4) If the retrial is initiated by the People's Court ex officio and there is no petitioner, the plaintiff or appellant in the original trial shall first make a statement, followed by the opinions of other parties in the original trial.
For the circumstances specified in Items (1) to (3) of the preceding paragraph, the People's Court shall require the parties to clarify their retrial requests.

Article 406 During the retrial proceedings, if any of the following circumstances exists, the retrial procedure may be ruled to be terminated:
(1) The applicant for retrial withdraws the retrial request during the retrial, and the People's Court permits it;
(2) The applicant for retrial, after being summoned by subpoena, fails to appear in court without justifiable reasons, or leaves the courtroom midway without the court's permission, and it is handled as a withdrawal of the retrial request;
(3) The People's Procuratorate withdraws its protest;
(4) The circumstances specified in Items (1) to (4) of Article 402 of this Interpretation exist.
For a case where a retrial was ruled due to a protest filed by the People's Procuratorate, if the party applying for the protest falls under the circumstances specified in the preceding paragraph and it does not harm the national interest, social public interest, or the lawful rights and interests of others, the People's Court shall rule to terminate the retrial procedure.
After the termination of the retrial procedure, the execution of the original effective judgment that was suspended by a ruling of the People's Court shall be automatically resumed.

Article 416 If a local People's Procuratorate at any level, upon the application of a party, submits a retrial procuratorial recommendation to a People's Court at the same level against an effective judgment or ruling, it shall be accepted provided that the following conditions are met:
(1) The written retrial procuratorial recommendation, the application of the party in the original trial, and the relevant evidence materials have been submitted;
(2) The object of the recommendation for retrial is a judgment or ruling that may be retried in accordance with the Civil Procedure Law and this Interpretation;
(3) The written retrial procuratorial recommendation specifies that the judgment or ruling falls under the circumstances prescribed in Article 208, Paragraph 2 of the Civil Procedure Law;
(4) The circumstances specified in Article 209, Paragraph 1, Items (1) and (2) of the Civil Procedure Law are satisfied;
(5) The retrial procuratorial recommendation has been discussed and decided by the Procuratorial Committee of that People's Procuratorate.
If the provisions of the preceding paragraph are not satisfied, the People's Court may suggest that the People's Procuratorate make corrections or withdraw the recommendation; if no correction or withdrawal is made, the People's Court shall notify the People's Procuratorate in writing that the recommendation shall not be accepted.

Article 417 If the People's Procuratorate, upon the application of a party, files a protest against an effective judgment or ruling, and the following conditions are met, the People's Court shall rule to conduct a retrial within thirty days:
(1) The written protest, the application of the party in the original trial, and the relevant evidence materials have been submitted;
(2) The object of the protest is a judgment or ruling that may be retried in accordance with the Civil Procedure Law and this Interpretation;
(3) The written protest specifies that the judgment or ruling falls under the circumstances prescribed in Article 208, Paragraph 1 of the Civil Procedure Law;
(4) The circumstances specified in Article 209, Paragraph 1, Items (1) and (2) of the Civil Procedure Law are satisfied.
If the provisions of the preceding paragraph are not satisfied, the People's Court may suggest that the People's Procuratorate make corrections or withdraw the protest; if no correction or withdrawal is made, the People's Court may rule not to accept the protest.

Article 429 If the creditor's application for a payment order meets the following conditions, the Primary People's Court shall accept it and notify the creditor within five days after receiving the application:
(1) The claim is for payment of money or negotiable instruments such as bills of exchange, promissory notes, checks, stocks, bonds, treasury bonds, and transferable certificates of deposit;
(2) The money or negotiable instruments claimed have become due and the amount is definite, and the facts and evidence on which the claim is based are stated;
(3) The creditor has no countervailing obligation;
(4) The debtor is within the territory of China and is not missing;
(5) The payment order can be served on the debtor;
(6) The People's Court that received the application has jurisdiction;
(7) The creditor has not applied to the People's Court for pre-litigation preservation.
If the provisions of the preceding paragraph are not satisfied, the People's Court shall notify the creditor within five days after receiving the application that the application shall not be accepted.
When a Primary People's Court accepts a case for application for a payment order, it shall not be subject to any limitation on the amount of the creditor's claim.

Article 430 After accepting an application, the People's Court shall have a single judge conduct a review. Upon review, if any of the following circumstances exists, it shall rule to dismiss the application:
(1) The applicant does not have the capacity as a party;
(2) The documentary evidence for payment of money or negotiable instruments does not stipulate interest for overdue payment or liquidated damages/compensation, but the creditor insists on claiming such interest or damages;
(3) The money or negotiable instruments claimed are illegal proceeds;
(4) The money or negotiable instruments claimed have not yet become due or the amount is uncertain.
If, after accepting an application for a payment order, the People's Court finds that the conditions for acceptance under this Interpretation are not satisfied, it shall rule to dismiss the application within fifteen days from the date of acceptance.

Article 432 If any of the following circumstances exists, the People's Court shall rule to terminate the summary procedure, and if a payment order has been issued, the payment order shall become void automatically:
(1) After the People's Court accepts the application for a payment order, the creditor files a lawsuit on the same debtor-creditor relationship;
(2) The payment order cannot be served on the debtor within thirty days from the date of its issuance;
(3) Before the debtor receives the payment order, the creditor withdraws the application.

Article 437 Upon formal review, if the written objection raised by the debtor falls under any of the following circumstances, it shall be determined that the objection is established, the procedure shall be ruled to be terminated, and the payment order shall become void automatically:
(1) Circumstances where the application shall not be accepted as prescribed in this Interpretation;
(2) Circumstances where the application shall be dismissed by ruling as prescribed in this Interpretation;
(3) Circumstances where the summary procedure shall be ruled to be terminated as prescribed in this Interpretation;
(4) The People's Court reasonably doubts whether the conditions for issuing a payment order are met.

Article 447 The announcement of acceptance of the application issued by the People's Court in accordance with Article 219 of the Civil Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China shall specify the following:
(1) the name of the applicant for public summons for exhortation;
(2) the type, number, face amount, drawer, endorser, holder, payment term, and other particulars of the instrument, as well as the type, number, scope of rights, right holder, obligor, exercise date, and other particulars of other negotiable instruments for which an application for public summons for exhortation may be made;
(3) the period for claiming rights;
(4) the legal consequences of transferring the instrument or other negotiable instruments during the period of public summons for exhortation and the failure of an interested person to claim rights.

Article 460 The legitimate reasons prescribed in Article 223 of the Civil Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China include:
(1) the interested person is unable to know the fact of the announcement due to an accident or force majeure;
(2) the interested person is unable to know the fact of the announcement because his personal freedom is restricted, or although he knows the fact of the announcement, he is unable to claim rights himself or to entrust another person to claim rights on his behalf;
(3) the circumstances do not fall within the statutory situations for applying for public summons for exhortation;
(4) no announcement is made or the announcement is not made in the statutory manner;
(5) other objective reasons that cause the interested person to fail to claim rights in the People's Court before the judgment is rendered.

Article 522 The People's Court may determine a case as a foreign-related civil case under any of the following circumstances:
(1) one or both of the parties are foreigners, stateless persons, foreign enterprises, or organizations;
(2) the habitual residence of one or both of the parties is outside the territory of the People's Republic of China;
(3) the subject matter is located outside the territory of the People's Republic of China;
(4) the legal facts that create, change, or extinguish a civil relationship occur outside the territory of the People's Republic of China;
(5) other circumstances that may be determined as a foreign-related civil case.

Article 532 If a foreign-related civil case concurrently meets the following circumstances, the People's Court may rule to dismiss the plaintiff's action and inform the plaintiff to bring an action in a more convenient foreign court:
(1) the defendant raises a request that the case should be under the jurisdiction of a more convenient foreign court, or raises an objection to jurisdiction;
(2) there is no agreement between the parties choosing the jurisdiction of a court of the People's Republic of China;
(3) the case does not fall under the exclusive jurisdiction of a court of the People's Republic of China;
(4) the case does not involve the interests of the state, citizens, legal persons, or other organizations of the People's Republic of China;
(5) the main facts in dispute in the case do not occur within the territory of the People's Republic of China, and the case is not governed by the law of the People's Republic of China, and the People's Court would have significant difficulties in ascertaining the facts and applying the law in trying the case;
(6) a foreign court has jurisdiction over the case and it would be more convenient for that court to hear the case.
